target/
.DS_Store
.claude/
CLAUDE.md
sipnab-x86_64-linux

# Editor artifacts
*.swp
*.swo
*~

# VHS intermediate output for the hero tape (PNG is the committed asset)
demos/.hero-tmp.gif
__pycache__/

# Built Zola site (regenerated by pages.yml CI; never hand-committed)
website/public/

# Generated wiki tree (scripts/build-wiki.py output; wiki-sync.yml publishes it)
build/

# Subagent-driven-development scratch: ledgers, task briefs, review packages.
# Ephemeral coordination state; the git history is the durable record.
.superpowers/

# Vale's third-party style packages, fetched by `vale sync` from the
# `Packages =` line in .vale.ini. Not vendored: they are upstream rules that
# would go stale in review, and the CI job syncs them before linting. What IS
# committed is this project's own rules and vocabulary under
# .vale/styles/sipnab and .vale/styles/config.
.vale/styles/Google/

# LLVM coverage profile data. `cargo llvm-cov` and any RUSTFLAGS carrying
# -Cinstrument-coverage drop these in the working directory, and 45 of them
# (38 MB) reached the repository because nothing here excluded them and
# `git add -A` is indiscriminate. They are build output, regenerated on every
# coverage run, and useless to anyone cloning.
*.profraw
*.profdata

# Agent/debug scratch written into the repo root. `orph.json` appeared during a
# validation run holding real capture IPs — the corpus at /srv/pcaps
# carries PII and nothing derived from it may reach the repository. This is the
# same shape as the 45 .profraw files that reached GitHub: a stray artefact plus
# an indiscriminate `git add -A`. Scratch belongs outside the tree; this is the
# backstop for when it does not.
/orph.json
/*.tmp.json

# Benchmark corpora. bench/README.md tells operators to write corpus.pcap and
# sweep-<N>.pcap into the working directory, and bench/live-capture.sh generates
# its own corpus — 138 MB for the headline run, gigabytes at the top of the rate
# ladder. Same shape as the .profraw files above: a large stray artefact plus an
# indiscriminate `git add -A`, except this one is also capture-shaped, so a
# reviewer skimming a diff has no way to tell a synthetic corpus from a real one.
# Anchored with a leading `/` on purpose: 31 .pcap and .pcapng fixtures under
# tests/, fuzz/corpus/ and harness/ are TRACKED, and an unanchored `*.pcap` would
# make the next one un-addable without `git add -f`.
/*.pcap
/*.pcapng
/bench/*.pcap
/bench/*.pcapng

# The browser analyzer binary. Built by .github/workflows/pages.yml at
# deploy time — see the "THE COMMITTED BUNDLE IS NO LONGER WHAT SHIPS" note
# there. Committing it served eleven releases of stale analysis and tripped
# the OpenSSF Scorecard Binary-Artifacts check, which is right: a binary in a
# repository cannot be audited from source.
website/static/wasm/sipnab_bg.wasm

# Gate transcripts: one run on one machine, absolute paths and all.
.git-docsgate.log
*.log
